<section xml:id="sec_conductors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Conductors</title>

	<activity>
		<title>Warm-Up</title>
		<introduction>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>The diagram shows a large positive charge next to an electrical conductor.  A conductor is a material that has a lot of free charges that can move around in response to external fields: most metals are good electrical conductors.  The conductor in the figure is neutral, so its <em>net charge</em> is zero.</p>

		<figure xml:id="conductor-img">
			  <title>Conductor Figure</title>
			<caption>A large positive charge to the left of a conductor.</caption>
			<image source="Conductor.png" width = "50%" ><description>A long rod is positively charged and is held next to a conductor represented as a square.</description></image>
		</figure>
	</introduction>
		<task>
			
			<p><alert>Predict</alert> how you expect the charges in the conductor to distribute themselves in the presence of the large positive charge.  Make a charge diagram showing your prediction.</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>Together, the large positive charge and the charges in the conductor will create a net electric field everywhere in space.  Predict the direction of this net electric field at the <em>center</em> of the conductor.</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>Click on <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqvImbn9GG4" visual="this link"/> to view a video demonstrating what happens in case like the one described above.</p>
		</task>
		<task>
		<p>Has your thinking about the electric field at the center of the conductor changed? If so, explain how and why it has changed.</p>
		</task>
	</activity>
		
