  <subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>
	
	<!--<p>Electrical conductors are extremely useful objects that interact in interesting ways with external electric fields.</p>-->

		<definition>
		<title>Electrical Conductor</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>An <term>electrical conductor</term> is an object that possesses free electric charges that can move in response to external electric fields.  When the conductor is in <xref ref="a_electrostatic-eq"/> it has the following properties:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<p>
							<alert>Inside a conductor <m>\vec{E} = 0</m>.</alert>  This is true for <em>every point</em> in the interior of the conductor.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<alert>The conductor is an equipotential surface.</alert>  For any two points inside the conductor, <m>\Delta V</m> must be equal to zero.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<alert>The external electric field is perpendicular to the surface.</alert>  This is true locally for the entire surface, but once you get some distance from the surface, the electric field can point in other directions.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<alert>All surplus charge exists at the surface.</alert>  All points inside the conductor, not at the surface, will have a net charge equal to zero.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<alert>Charge density is largest near corners.</alert>  As a result of this, the magnitude of the electric field will also be largest near corners.  The sharper the corner, the larger the charge density and the field.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
		</statement>
	</definition>

	<assumption xml:id="a_electrostatic-eq">
	<title>Electrostatic Equilibrium</title>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>A conductor is in electrostatic equilibrium when no charges within the conductor are moving, and usually occurs very quickly after an external electric field is applied to the conductor.  If there is an <em>internal electric field</em>, the conductor is <alert>not</alert> in electrostatic equilibrium.</p>
			
</assumption>
	


	

